# 026 · Ship v0.1.0

**Status:** `READY` · **Version:** [v0.1.0](../roadmap.md#v010) · **Part:** The release · **Depends on:** [001](001-plans-in-public.goal), [002](002-ci-and-repository-settings.goal), [003](003-advisors-no-default-cap.goal), [004](004-secret-scan.goal), [005](005-sample-project.goal), [007](007-known-limits.goal), [008](008-community-files.goal), [009](009-subscription-sign-in-tests.goal), [010](010-published-images.goal), [011](011-release-bundle.goal), [012](012-version-checks.goal), [013](013-thermite-doctor.goal), [014](014-quick-start-in-ci.goal), [015](015-log-paging.goal), [016](016-small-fixes.goal), [017](017-legacy-names.goal), [018](018-runpod-spike.goal), [019](019-rig-only-entry.goal), [020](020-runpod-rig-image.goal), [021](021-enrollment-codes.goal), [022](022-runpod-launch.goal), [023](023-runpod-lifecycle.goal), [024](024-runpod-models-and-results.goal), [025](025-runpod-tests-and-docs.goal), [056](056-codex-harness.goal), [060](060-docs-site.goal), [062](062-research-folder.goal), [063](063-scan-and-steer.goal), [064](064-experiment-queue.goal), [065](065-journals-and-corpus.goal), [066](066-direction-rules.goal), [067](067-assign-queue-from.goal), [068](068-mcp-servers-for-agents.goal)

## What

When every other v0.1.0 goal is verified:

1. Test the quick start on clean machines, from the release bundle and from source: Linux with Docker, macOS with Docker Desktop, and macOS with Podman.
2. Run the full checks and the acceptance suites the release touches, once.
3. Open a pull request into `main`. Merge it when CI passes.
4. Write the changelog.
5. Tag `v0.1.0`. The tag publishes the images.
6. Publish the GitHub Release, with notes on what works, the known limits and how to report a bug.

## Why

This is the first release that people outside the project can install and test.

## Steps for the owner

The owner merges into `main` and makes the tag (2026-10-20: "I will handle merging to main." and "We aren't ready for version tag imo."). When the owner says the project is ready:

1. **Check what is left.** The release plan lists each goal's state; the goals left open say what each waits for. Run the full check (`npm run check`) and the acceptances the last changes need on the tip of `v1/build`.
2. **Date the changelog.** In `CHANGELOG.md`, rename `[Unreleased]: v0.1.0, the first public release` to `[0.1.0] - <date>`, and commit it on `v1/build`.
3. **Move `main`.** `main` follows `v1/build` as a fast-forward: `git fetch . v1/build:main`, then `git push origin v1/build main`. Cloudflare Pages rebuilds the docs site from `main`.
4. **Tag.** On `main`: `git tag -a v0.1.0 -m "Thermite v0.1.0"` and `git push origin v0.1.0`. The release workflow builds and publishes the images (control, the CPU and CUDA harnesses, the supervisor and the rig image), writes the release bundle (`compose.yaml` pinned by digest, and its README as the notes) and attaches it to a draft release.
5. **Check the packages.** Each image must pull without signing in: `docker pull` each `ghcr.io/…:0.1.0` from a machine that is signed out. A package that was never public before (control's, the supervisor's) may need its visibility set to public once, in the repository's packages.
6. **Publish.** Read the draft release's notes (what is in the release, the known limits, how to report a problem, the images), change what needs it, and publish it.
7. **Test from the release.** On a clean machine, follow [Your first run](../../_site/src/content/docs/tutorials/first-run.md) from the published bundle.

## Done when

- [ ] `main` holds the release, and the tag and the images exist.
- [ ] The release notes are published.

## Decisions

- 2026-10-19, owner: The first public release is v0.1.0. It holds the tester basics, published images and rigs on RunPod.
- 2026-10-19, owner: The code moves to `main` only as the last step before the release.
- 2026-10-20, owner: The code reaches `main` through a pull request merged as a fast-forward, as recommended.
- 2026-10-20, owner: The clean-machine test on macOS with Docker Desktop is deferred. Linux with Docker runs on a fresh virtual machine; macOS with Podman runs as planned.
- 2026-10-21, owner: The test on macOS with Docker Desktop must pass before the tag. This replaces the deferral above.
- 2026-10-21, owner: The judge's smoke check passes, with a note that the machine's timing is noisy, when the no-op change is its only inconclusive result.
- 2026-10-20, owner: The owner merges the code into `main` and makes the tag. The project is not ready for a version tag yet.
- 2026-10-20, owner: The code moved to `main` early, at the development branch's state that day, so the docs site can build from `main` (goal 060). Work stays on the development branch, and `main` follows it. The release is still this goal's pull request and tag.

## Open questions

None.

## Progress

- 2026-10-19: Goal written from the v0.1.0 release plan.
- 2026-10-20: Depends on every v0.1.0 goal in the release plan, goals 001, 060 and 062 to 068 included; goal 006 was dropped into 060.
- 2026-10-20: `CHANGELOG.md` drafted under Unreleased from what is built, and linked from the README. Bring it up to date, and give it the version and date, at the tag.
- 2026-10-21: The release notes are the bundle's README (`containers/release/README.template.md`); it now also says what is in the release (the changelog at the tag), the known limits and how to report a problem, and names the third-party programs in the harness images. The changelog has the latest work. The steps for the owner are above.
- 2026-10-21: The quick start on a clean Linux machine (a Debian 13 VM with Docker 29) went through install, start, sign-in and the isolation check, then failed twice at the judge's smoke check: the no-op change came out `inconclusive` instead of `neutral`, as the smoke check reports when a machine's timing is too noisy (the VM shares its host). A decision for the owner: whether the smoke check passes, with a note on noisy timing, when only the no-op is inconclusive.
- 2026-10-21: Built the owner's answer. The judge's smoke check passes when the only wrong result is the no-op's `inconclusive` in place of `neutral`; its summary then ends with the note `The timing on this machine is noisy. Results here need more repeats.`, which the `rig_check_finished` event and control's view of the rig also carry (`note`). Any other wrong result, alone or beside that one, still fails it (`src/harness/smoke-check.ts`, tested in `smoke-check.test.ts`, `service.test.ts` and `rig-page.test.ts`). Pages: Your first run (step 6), Check a rig, Troubleshoot (Judge smoke passed with a note) and the rig checks reference (Noisy timing). The quick-start acceptance still expects the page's clean pass and its verdict counts, so a noisy machine still fails it there. Next: the quick start on the clean Linux machine again.

